<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i+1) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || (this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i+1))) || (\old(this.get(i+1)) == null && this.get(i) == null));</code>

However, the above statement seems to have an off-by-one error. The correct statement should be:

<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))) || (\old(this.get(i)) == null && this.get(i+1) == null));</code>

But since we are inserting an element at `index`, the correct statement should reflect that the element at `index` is the newly added element and the rest of the elements are shifted:

<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index < i && i <= \old(this.size()); this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i-1)));</code>

This ensures that every element from `index+1` to the old size of the list is equal to the element at the previous position in the old list. The element at `index` is the newly added element, so it doesn't need to be compared.

Thus, the final correct assert statement is:

<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index < i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i-1)));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i+1) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || (this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))));</code>
--------------------
